Yes — I see it now. I over-compressed D O U C into an acronym and missed that the typography itself was the payload. ;)

Assuming ordinary block capitals, each letter is a little symmetry experiment:

* D — primarily horizontal reflection symmetry, but with asymmetric left/right roles: rigid stem versus curved boundary. Symmetric across one axis, yet structurally polarized.
* O — the high-symmetry case: horizontal + vertical reflection, 180° rotational symmetry, and if idealized as a circle, effectively continuous rotational symmetry. Lots of equivalent orientations.
* U — primarily vertical reflection symmetry. Left and right exchange cleanly, while top and bottom absolutely do not.
* C — approximately horizontal reflection symmetry, but unlike D it is open. Same rough axis can therefore yield a different affordance because closure/topology changed.

So you weren’t merely saying:

\text{more symmetry}\rightarrow\text{more affordance}

You were showing several species of symmetry side-by-side:

D\quad O\quad U\quad C

and letting the eye perform the comparison.

The especially useful bit is D vs C. They can share roughly the same reflection axis while having different topology:

\text{D = closed boundary}

\text{C = interrupted/open boundary}

Therefore:

\boxed{
\text{same symmetry axis}
\not\Rightarrow
\text{same affordance}
}

Symmetry needs a richer description than a count.

Something like a Symmetry Signature:

\boxed{
\mathcal S(X)
=
(
\text{axes},
\text{transform types},
\text{degree},
\text{topology},
\text{fixed points},
\text{broken regions}
)
}

Then:

\text{Affordance}(X)
=
F(\mathcal S(X),C)

rather than simply F(\#\text{ symmetries}).

And there’s another nice layer: O has so much symmetry that orientation becomes poorly encoded, while D/U/C contain asymmetries that tell you which way is “up,” “left,” “open,” etc.

So your four letters demonstrate the principle themselves:

Symmetry determines what transformations are cheap; symmetry-breaking determines what distinctions are readable.

Which is very Pantheon-reader territory.

